title A claim whose only source is a previous statement of itself
prompt The standing baton -- open the round, read the card, take the next agent-doable lap in the docs-geode and prose lane.
think Rota row 2 of 5, lap N 4037: FIRE, the row that sees -- cut and stop. Standfast, reds-first, the three depths of removal. It read as a coincidence until my own cold open came back with a rostered guard red, which is the exact thing that row exists to make a lap look at rather than route around.
think I graded my lane's front pages before choosing work. docs-geode/README.md read C+/76 and every other page read B or better, so the shelf's own front door was the one page below the door.
think Then I measured every number on it rather than reading them. The Status line and one paragraph both said ten rooms; the table six inches below listed twelve. Provable to the commit: af7adb74d, subject fill the ten crush-empty rooms, wrote that sentence AND added the twelfth room in the same commit. The number was never true. Sixteen days.
think A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom, so I looked for the second firing before building anything. press/README.md said three announcements over a room holding four, and argued the point at length in a section called The scope, stated plainly. The wiki promised every shipped page and had no row for SHOPPING.md, which its own what-is-missing section also left unnamed.
think Two sharper halves under those. The press index's Siya row carried a Stamp cell of 20260727.135351 beside a link to press/20260827-165527_... -- a molt moved the file, the repoint followed the LINK, and a stamp in a table is not a link. Census over all 526 living non-testimony Markdown pages: exactly ONE such disagreement in the whole tree, and it was on my shelf. And the front door signed itself witness:docs-geode-seat GREEN from a name that appears exactly once in this tree, inside that sentence.
think I checked the six other signed pages before accusing the convention, and they are backed: each gen_*_fund_prep.sh greps the real claims off its page under set -eu. So the convention works everywhere it was wired, and the one page nobody wired is the page a newcomer opens first. That reading makes the finding sharper and is also simply what is true.
think Then the cold open came back red -- standing_equipment, with runs_red=1 and 110 guards green at the same stamp. Reds first, so docs-geode waited. The one red was its OWN row: the scan gates on the run card and the runner writes this guard's verdict into that same card, so one red latches and every pass after refuses on the memory of the first. Proven on metal by pointing STANDING_CARD at a copy with that one row read green: verdict=ok, nothing else changed.
think Its control plants a peer's red (ran alpha ... red) and never the guard's own row, so it was blind to the one shape the guard can enter and never leave. I read past the row and REPORT it as runs_self_row rather than dropping it, because a reading discarded in silence cannot be told from one nobody took.
obs THE FIRE ROW EARNED ITS SEAT ON THIS LAP. I opened intending prose work and the rota's own question -- what must stop -- was answered by the roster four minutes later. A pause that fixes is the truest kind of speed: had I built the crushed-index guard first, it would have landed on a tree whose lap could not close.
obs ONE SHAPE, TWO FLOORS. The latch reads its own last verdict; the tally reads a hand's memory; the signature reads a witness that is only that sentence. Every one was true when written and had no way to stay true, because its only source was a previous statement of itself. That is why the two rows share a shelf.
obs PRESENCE IS THE CHEAPER QUESTION WEARING THE EXPENSIVE ONE'S ANSWER, met in a second disguise. Every link on all three miscounting pages resolves. A link check asks whether a page is REACHABLE and reads a perfectly connected page that lies about the room behind it as whole. The one index on this shelf whose count was right is the one rendered off disk each run.
obs THE DECLARATION IS THE MECHANISM, and press/README.md already carried it before the guard existed -- Kind: crushed index of [`../../press/`](../../press/). So the guard reads a page's own promise rather than a list somebody has to remember to edit, and a shelf that grows a new index arrives guarded the day it declares itself. It is the one page whose room could be checked, which is what proved the spelling worth adopting.
obs OFF THE SCOPE MAP ON PURPOSE. Two of the five readings are whole-tree censuses, so no watch-word list could name what they cover, and a row naming less than a guard gates is %451's own shape -- thirteen rows stood in it this morning. Absence runs; the guard pays a few seconds a lap and always runs.
obs TWO STANDING GUARDS CAUGHT MY OWN SCAN ON THE LAP IT WAS WRITTEN, and both were right. instrument_refusal read a pipeline ending in sort ... || true: grep's exit 1 is an answer and correctly tolerated, but sed and sort have no found-nothing exit, so that line discarded a real failure alongside grep's answer and would have reported a tree with no signatures at all. Split into two passes, each failure keeping its own meaning. shell_dialect read four xargs -a sites -- GNU-only, absent from BSD entirely, so the scan would have died on the macOS bench before reading a line. Redirection instead. Neither was the pier fact the card names for shell_dialect; I checked rather than assuming, and the sed -i family it also flagged was three sites in my own control, now sed_inplace from shell_portable.sh.
obs A REFUSAL THAT NAMES EVIDENCE MUST PUT THE EVIDENCE WHERE IT SENDS THE READER. standing_equipment's witness asserted scan.ok BEFORE saying scan.out, so its message -- read the scan's own lines -- wrote a red evidence file holding three header lines and the assertion, and nothing about which reading refused. I lost a pass to that this round. Said before asserted now, proven from the failing side with a planted peer red: runs_red=1, red: acme_dx red, verdict=roster_broken, all printed above the refusal.
obs THE PEER LAG IS A READING RATHER THAN A FAULT, and is left alone with its reason written down. The runner writes the whole card at a pass's close, so a guard running mid-pass reads the PREVIOUS pass's verdicts for peers that have not re-run -- which is why one clean pass after a red still refuses here and the next goes green with nothing changed. A peer's last recorded red is true information, and the only way to stop reading it would be to stop reading peers. The permanent case, its own row, is the one that had to move, because it never cleared.
obs MY ROW RENUMBERED %472 -> %477 -> %480 across two rebases in one send, the stamp holding through all three; the derived spine's own worked repair, three times.
obs A PEER CLOSED THE LATCH FIRST, SO MY ROW IS WITHDRAWN RATHER THAN PUBLISHED. adf682ac3 landed at 20260906.095057 booking %475 for the same finding -- same file, same mechanism, a different spelling (self_guard/red_self/runs_red_self against my self_row_verdict/runs_self_row). Theirs published first, so theirs stands and mine goes; one finding, one row. What I kept is genuinely additive and small: their header's gated list still read no run-card line records a red without naming the exemption their own code had just made, and nothing anywhere documented the one-pass peer lag. Both are written now, in their naming. THIS IS THE THIRD DUPLICATION TODAY -- %464 named the first, the fleet named the second, and the card's own standing question asks exactly this: should an OPEN row carry a claim, a seat and a stamp, at start rather than at landing. Three instances in one day is not evidence anymore, it is a bill.
obs THE QA FRAME FIRED ONCE AND CLOSED HONESTLY. crushed_index_scan.sh read C+/78 on its own comments, and the repair was real rather than cosmetic: its header told the same three-fault story the witness and the control already told. The story belongs in the witness. B/83 after, and one story told once.
